Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.709 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this is an artist painting a portrait of  a cat  she wasn't hired by this cat's owner but  by chewie  the online pet supply store the company  has sent out tens of thousands of these  surprise pet portraits over the past  eight years and during the pandemic  chewie customers have shared them across  social media  at the same time the company's business  has been booming  in 2020 chewie gained over five and a  half million  active customers we took action pretty  quickly actually in our opinion  and the strategy looking back was really  communicate  innovate and persevere here's how chewie  seized the pandemic pet craze  and ignited investor enthusiasm and why  some analysts think its success is built  to last  when the pandemic took hold in 2020  millions of people were forced to work  from home  and with more time on their hands they  adopted new pets  analysts estimate that more than 11  million pets found new homes during the  pandemic  and while pet owners were stuck at home  many turned to the internet to buy  supplies  according to a recent report consumers  bought 30 percent of their pet food and  supplies online last year  up from 23 in 2019 this added up to  about a five billion dollar increase in  online pet spending  lauren schenck who analyzes the pet  industry for morgan stanley  says this increase was a big benefit for  retailers  we do believe that the pet industry is  is better positioned than some other  e-commerce categories to retain  the gains that it saw in 2020 given the  sticky like nature of not only the  product  and pet food but also the customers  themselves which tend to be very loyal  to specific  retailers with the influx of new pet  owners  retailers like target and amazon all  gained more shoppers over the past year  but sitting at the top of the dog pile  chewy  morgan stanley says the company has the  number one market share position for  online pet retail  chewy has performed exceptionally well  during  the pandemic obviously a beneficiary of  the shift from offline to online as it  relates to e-commerce  but they also benefited from the  structural increase in pet ownership  but chewy success wasn't just timing  analysts say it had a lot to do with how  management at the company  handled the demand from the pandemic pet  boom when covid19 first took hold in the  u.s  in march 2020 chewie like many other  companies  was overwhelmed with orders the surge in  e-commerce demand  strained postal services and this caused  week-long delays that put even more  pressure on retailers  sumit singh the ceo of chewie explains  how this  impacted his company when industry-wide  supply chains were disrupted what was  going on was that we weren't receiving  products optimally across the  fulfillment center  number two because fulfillment center  backlog was so high  typically we deliver product between one  and two days to customers reliably  our shipping times went up to seven to  ten days competitors like amazon  face similar issues but at this key  moment  chewie made strategic decisions to  handle the increase in demand  it hired thousands of new workers while  protecting shipments for its core  subscription customers  the company also invested in shipping  packages by air to ensure timely  delivery  across a combination of these three  mechanisms we were able to sort of  control our situation  while these investments were expensive  shanks says they were the right move  for businesses that can retain these  customers which we think chewy can  this should ultimately pay off dividends  over the course of the next several  years  over the past year chewie rolled out a  number of new products  with a focus on pet health in october  the company released its own pet  telehealth service called  connect with a vet the company's ceo  says the platform was launched in  response to the demand for vet care  while clinics were impacted by global  shutdowns we internally said okay  how about we develop a solution that  allows us to connect customers and  veterinarians  in a time of crisis and we did exactly  that chewy continued to lean into its  healthcare business  the company announced that it would be  manufacturing its own compounded  medications  which analysts say is a competitive  advantage but rolling out new products  is expensive  and this cut into chewy's profits  analysts say these product investments  along with other pandemic related  expenses are part of why the company has  yet to show an annual profit  anytime you make an investment uh yes  you're committing to  uh you know generating a healthy return  over the long term and we are very  long-term focused  as it incidentally happens this  particular service connect with a vet  yes it is currently a cost center  because we're not monetizing it it's  free  for customers still analysts say some  investors have concerns  one of the things that investors have  also been a little bit concerned about  is the fact that chewy's flow through  has been smaller or lighter than some  other e-commerce retailers experienced  during the pandemic and i think part of  that reason was  the incremental cleaning costs and the  shipping costs but also the fact that  they did open a new distribution center  the company's stock saw pullback over  recent weeks  the cost that we've invested in our  health in the health and safety of our  team members  we are not looking to roll those costs  back uh you know up  100 we believe 80 of those costs will  actually remain in network in 2021. once  inventory shortages  are more or inventory balances are  better in check and control  we believe that logistics costs and  inventory freight fulfillment cost and  all of that stuff  will actually start becoming uh you know  a little less of a concern  despite challenges during the pandemic  analysts say the future of the company  looks positive  on march 30th chewie reported its first  quarterly net profit ever  along with a 47 increase in sales over  the past year  analysts expect chewy to have its first  full year of profitability in 2021  but they say that its rate of growth  will likely slow down  this was a major structural shift in  e-commerce penetration that is  we think unlikely to revert so we do  think 2021 e-commerce growth decelerates  versus what we saw in 2020  but we don't think you actually give  back any of the share shift so we do  think this is  structural this is permanent experts say  the growth  e-commerce companies like chewie have  experienced are likely to be the most  significant and lasting effects of the  pandemic on the pet care industry

